#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120625} :activity/id #uuid "faea29b6-21e9-5455-abc1-ad6fe37387f0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121020 :tutorial/content "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations:\n\nI. Swapping two rows\n\nII. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\nIII. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix $A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Taking the determinant, we have $$\\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1 \\cdot 4 - 2 \\cdot 3 = -2.$$\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =2 \\\\ & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =-4 \\\\ & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\ & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5ae8e63-4d2c-5491-9908-96fe94243ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57d74627-70e0-5830-ba95-6cb026f4de4b" :step/math-academy-id 16213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119589 :question/id #uuid "596aa514-d598-5ffe-affe-f5580d130bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385557}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"} :step/id #uuid "07d38f85-837d-577c-a3fa-ed4adb824e28" :step/math-academy-id 16214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120704}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119590 :question/id #uuid "89152146-8f04-5b4f-8436-15b82431c35d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4423"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f11b223-edd2-59b8-b761-164d56e29746" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383181}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Row by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "b2bed0ab-d12e-5d90-bbf5-94e480bceb4c" :step/math-academy-id 16215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119591 :question/id #uuid "6d46c442-4675-5e0e-a834-a8b10ad2b86a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24e5e193-5e30-5480-9e5d-b7b8f1d5de87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385571}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Applying Several Elementary Operations"} :step/id #uuid "9c7f038a-e114-5e98-bc36-49762d4031f6" :step/math-academy-id 16216 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121021 :tutorial/content "Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A),$ the corresponding elementary column operations on determinants have the same properties as the elementary row ones.\n\n- Swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant.\n\n- Multiplying a column by a number scales the determinant by that number.\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column does not change the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "bac0b72b-b76a-5a06-9312-d2b7b8ac4247" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3005 :tutorial/title "Column Operations on Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "d5da1bd5-0cc1-56fe-a8d7-36aa6576326a" :step/math-academy-id 16217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121044 :tutorial/content "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=-det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f151b612-1bdb-5723-af50-a48439141fbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3236 :tutorial/title "Switching Two Rows Changes the Sign of the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "1889d210-a2bc-5893-b7e3-879d8fc8961f" :step/math-academy-id 17635 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121045 :tutorial/content "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have $$\\left| \\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09c28da6-5340-56f9-bbf5-427a824977ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3237 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Row By a Number Scales the Determinant By That Number"} :step/id #uuid "0cc1f27c-f5d8-5b74-9602-32ce4d87b799" :step/math-academy-id 17658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119665 :question/id #uuid "cbd9b4bc-146a-57f7-a03f-f045f86f3c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-6143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a0ab53e-79ff-5520-bb50-069fd381d29d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385442}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Row Operation Given the Connection Between Two Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "e1ec39b0-859a-5c8a-a8ca-f8a0f6371e76" :step/math-academy-id 22905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121061 :tutorial/content "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\\left| \\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = {\\color{red}3}^2 \\cdot \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}$$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c99dfd1-b583-5247-9ac5-c2ada47c0ee9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3880 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix By a Scalar"} :step/id #uuid "ebb7b384-6a1f-56e3-9927-8911a4ec3563" :step/math-academy-id 22922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119666 :question/id #uuid "ce910ef2-c3af-5173-84d4-ee2a4c957954" :question/math-academy-id "e-6148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "272d9a54-ba97-5175-86df-0a78337da7b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383195}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar"} :step/id #uuid "6bb5981c-60d1-56ed-a146-3ef4d9c0f6bb" :step/math-academy-id 22923 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120628}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119994}]}
